window.docContents[91].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___8":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.8. Induced representations\tSection 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction","header":"Section 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___8"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Category/#Chapter7___Definition7___1___1":{"contents":"Definition 7.1.1. A category \\mathcal{C} is the following data:\n\n(i) A class of objects Ob(\\mathcal{C}).\n(ii) For every objects X, Y \\in Ob(\\mathcal{C}), the class \\operatorname{Hom}_\\mathcal{C}(X, Y) = \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Y) of morphisms (or arrows) from X, Y (for f \\in \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Y), one may write f : X \\to Y).\n\n(iii) For any objects X, Y, Z \\in Ob(\\mathcal{C}), a composition map\n\n\n\\operatorname{Hom}(Y, Z) \\times \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Y) \\to \\operatorname{Hom}(X, Z), \\quad (f, g) \\mapsto f \\circ g.\n\n\nThis data is required to satisfy the following axioms:\n\n1. The composition is associative, i.e., (f \\circ g) \\circ h = f \\circ (g \\circ h).2. For each X \\in Ob(\\mathcal{C}), there is a morphism 1_X \\in \\operatorname{Hom}(X, X), called the unit morphism, such that 1_X \\circ f = f and g \\circ 1_X = g for any f, g for which compositions make sense.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.1. The definition of a category\tCategory","header":"Category","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___1___-The-definition-of-a-category/Category/#Chapter7___Definition7___1___1"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Column-orthogonality-of-characters/#Chapter4___Theorem4___5___4":{"contents":"Theorem 4.5.4. Let g, h \\in G, and let Z_g denote the centralizer of g in G. Then\n\n\\sum_V \\chi_V(g)\\overline{\\chi_V(h)} = \\begin{cases} |Z_g|, & \\text{if } g \\text{ is conjugate to } h, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise,} \\end{cases}\n\nwhere the summation is taken over all irreducible representations of G.\n\nProof. As noted above, \\overline{\\chi_V(h)} = \\chi_{V^*}(h), so the left-hand side equals (using Maschke's theorem):\n\n\\sum_V \\chi_V(g)\\chi_{V^*}(h) = \\operatorname{Tr}|_{\\bigoplus_V V \\otimes V^*}(g \\otimes (h^*)^{-1})\n\n= \\operatorname{Tr}|_{\\bigoplus_V \\operatorname{End} V}(x \\mapsto gxh^{-1}) = \\operatorname{Tr}|_{\\mathbb{C}[G]}(x \\mapsto gxh^{-1}).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.5. Orthogonality of characters\tColumn orthogonality of characters","header":"Column orthogonality of characters","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Column-orthogonality-of-characters/#Chapter4___Theorem4___5___4"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Weyl-algebra-as-algebra-of-polynomial-differential-operators/#Chapter2___Remark2___7___2":{"contents":"Remark 2.7.2. The proof of (i) shows that the Weyl algebra A can be viewed as the algebra of polynomial differential operators in one variable t.\n\nThe proof of (i) also brings up the notion of a faithful representation.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.7. Examples of algebras\tWeyl algebra as algebra of polynomial differential operators","header":"Weyl algebra as algebra of polynomial differential operators","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Weyl-algebra-as-algebra-of-polynomial-differential-operators/#Chapter2___Remark2___7___2"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/#chapter-04___section-4-8":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results","header":"4.8. Character tables, examples","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/#chapter-04___section-4-8"}});
